const body = `<h1>1.3b — Dice Roller Warmup</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Now that you've cracked the random formula, apply it. Create a file
        called <code>DiceTest.java</code>.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Part 1: Three Dice, One Roll</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Roll three d6 dice independently — each one gets its own
            <code>Math.random()</code> call.</li>
        <li>Calculate and print the sum (like a 3d6 stat roll in your RPG).</li>
        <li>Print each individual roll AND the total.</li>
    </ol>

    <pre><code><span class="output">Die 1: 4
Die 2: 6
Die 3: 2
Total: 12</span></code></pre>

    <p><strong>Part 2: Critical Detection</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Roll a d20.</li>
        <li>Print whether it's a "critical hit" (rolled 20) or "critical miss"
            (rolled 1) or a normal roll (anything else).</li>
        <li>You'll need <code>if</code> statements. Look at the QuickRef if you
            haven't seen them before.</li>
    </ol>

    <pre><code><span class="output">d20 roll: 20 — CRITICAL HIT!</span></code></pre>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Push It Further</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Roll a d100 (1–100). Use it to simulate a loot drop: print
                "You found a legendary sword!" if the roll is 95 or above,
                "Rare item!" if 70–94, and "Common loot." for anything below.</li>
            <li>How many <code>if</code> blocks did you need? Is there a cleaner
                way to structure them?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>
            You wrote the random formula on the previous page. But in
            <code>DiceTest.java</code>, you're writing it three times — once
            for each die. What if your RPG needs to roll dice in 20 different
            places? How could you avoid copying and pasting the formula every
            time? (We'll solve this in 1.7.)
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Write the random formula correctly from memory — every time</li>
            <li>Use <code>if</code> statements to check dice results</li>
            <li>Print formatted dice output with labels</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/math-random.html">Math.random()</a> — the four Tinker Challenges</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/printing.html">Printing &amp; Concatenation</a> — formatting dice output</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/three-step-pattern.html">Three-Step Pattern</a> — apply to your dice tests</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
